WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.962
Imagine a product sells for one hundred
dollars and requires sixty dollars of variable

2
00:00:04.962 --> 00:00:06.365
cost for each unit.

3
00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:09.432
The forty-dollar difference is its unit
contribution margin.

4
00:00:09.432 --> 00:00:11.319
It is not automatically profit.

5
00:00:11.319 --> 00:00:16.411
It is the amount that one additional sale
contributes toward fixed costs and, after

6
00:00:16.411 --> 00:00:18.229
those are covered, operating profit.

7
00:00:18.338 --> 00:00:22.866
Divide forty dollars by the one-hundred-dollar
selling price and the contribution margin

8
00:00:22.866 --> 00:00:24.375
ratio is forty percent.

9
00:00:24.375 --> 00:00:29.737
That means forty cents of each sales dollar is
available for fixed costs and then profit,

10
00:00:29.737 --> 00:00:31.077
under the model's assumptions.

11
00:00:31.077 --> 00:00:34.642
Ratios make it easier to compare products with
different prices.

12
00:00:34.754 --> 00:00:39.994
Managers use contribution margin to explore
break-even volume, product mix, and the effect

13
00:00:39.994 --> 00:00:42.412
of changing price or variable cost.

14
00:00:42.412 --> 00:00:45.514
The result is only as good as the assumptions.

15
00:00:45.514 --> 00:00:50.861
Costs may change outside a relevant range,
capacity may be limited, and demand may

16
00:00:50.861 --> 00:00:52.007
respond to price.

17
00:00:52.007 --> 00:00:56.497
Contribution margin is a decision model, not a
substitute for context.
